"A stream processing topology shows continuous event sources, operators, keyed state, and sinks.\n\nUse when: Review continuous transformations and stateful stream joins. Explain partitioning and delivery boundaries in a streaming application.\n\nAvoid when: A finite batch workflow or event-time interval alone is the focus.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes represent sources, processing operators, and sinks. Directed streams label event types and key changes. State stores and partition boundaries encode locality and recovery responsibility.\n\nBuild: Choose an application topology and input event contracts. Place operators in processing order and mark repartitioning. Attach state stores to the operators that own them. Route side outputs and error streams below the main path with unobstructed labels.\n\nCheck: Keying and partition assumptions support each stateful operation. Delivery and checkpoint guarantees are stated without unsupported exactly-once claims. Unbounded inputs and backpressure paths are identifiable. Streams, state connectors, and boundary titles do not intersect text.\n\nPitfalls: Confusing event-time order with arrival order. Drawing a broker as if it guarantees global ordering across partitions.\n\nExample: Click events are keyed by session, aggregated into activity summaries, and written to a serving store.\n\nAlternatives: Event-time window diagram, Publish-subscribe diagram."
